# **The deck of the JIB VI, as the boxes a person stands on.**
#
# Thirty-seven collision boxes and the instance node they hang off, in the
# boat's own frame. Nothing here is drawn.
# Instance this **as a child of a `jib_vi` instance** and the hull becomes
# walkable:
#
#   [[prefab]]
#   key = "jib_vi_decks"
#   path = "../prefabs/jib_vi_decks.loom"
#
#   [[node]]
#   name = "Decks"
#   parent = "<wherever the boat is>"
#   prefab = "jib_vi_decks"
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `assets/test/jib_vi_walk.loom` carried this block, and so did three other
# files, under a `ponytail:` note that said: *make it a `[[prefab]]` the first
# time a fifth wants it.* `assets/games/deeper_demo.loom` is the fifth, and it
# is the one that would hurt — a demo whose boat is boardable is not a test
# whose numbers can be re-derived from a header, and a hand-kept copy that
# drifted would put the player through the deck.
#
# **This file is now the original.** The four copies in `assets/test/` are not
# converted, deliberately: they carry three pinned determinism hashes between
# them, and a node-order change is exactly what would move one. Converting them
# is a commit whose whole content is "the hashes did not move", and it should
# be that and nothing else.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT A READER NEEDS TO KNOW BEFORE CHANGING A NUMBER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **These are collision only and only the plank is drawn.** The hull's own
# `BoxCollider` in `jib_vi.loom` is its *inertia*: its top face is at y = 1.66
# and the deck is at 0.60, so a capsule aboard used to stand on an invisible
# lid a metre in the air with the deckhouse, bulwarks and rails all passable.
# A `BoxCollider` on a descendant of a dynamic node is a collider on that body
# (ADR 0060) — and the moment one exists, `Sim::new` demotes the hull box to
# mass-only, which is what opens the deck up.
#
# **Every box weighs nothing** — `attach_box` builds them with
# `ColliderMassProps::Mass(0.0)`, resolving to exactly zero mass, zero angular
# inertia and no centre-of-mass shift. That is why `jib_vi.loom`'s stability
# arithmetic survives instancing this, and the three pinned hashes in
# `assets/test/` are its tripwire.
#
# **Boxes rather than a mesh-derived hull, deliberately.** A `voxelized_mesh`
# or a convex decomposition makes the sim hash a function of the art file, and
# the art file is still being detailed. Boxes track *architecture* — the sheer,
# the bulwark line, the deckhouse footprint, the flybridge — and those survive
# the mesh changing under them. The twelve `local_y` standing stations in
# `assets/test/jib_vi_stations.loom` are the detector for when they stop
# matching.
#
# The deck and bulwark numbers below were measured off `jib_vi_wide.obj` by
# marching columns through it. **That mesh is no longer what the prefab draws**
# — `jib_vi.loom` ships the refined twenty-one-material hull — so a number here
# that has not been re-marched against the shipped meshes is a number about a
# boat that is gone. The deckhouse note under this list was exactly that, and
# it cost the demo its interior. Everything added since is marched off
# `assets/meshes/jib_vi_*.obj` and says so where it lands.
#
#   deck at the centreline   0.66 aft, 0.60 amidships, 0.74 at x=4, 1.65 at x=8
#   walkable half-beam       2.55 aft, 2.80 amidships, 2.30 at x=4, 0.65 at x=8
#   bulwark cap              1.52 amidships rising to 2.45 at the stem
#   deckhouse footprint      x -6.2..3.9, |z| <= 1.50 (1.05 forward of x=2.2)
#   flybridge deck           3.08, x -6.5..4.2, |z| <= 2.60; helm console 3.45
#
# **THE DECKHOUSE USED TO BE SOLID AND THAT WAS A MEASUREMENT OF A DIFFERENT
# BOAT.** This file said "headroom under its canopy is 1.49 m and a capsule is
# 1.90 m, so the drawn floor in there is unreachable whatever the collider
# says", and it was true of `jib_vi_wide.obj`. The refined hull that replaced
# it has a real salon in there and a real door into it. Marched off the
# *shipped* meshes:
#
#   sole inside the house    0.60 aft, 0.62 amidships, 0.67 at x=2
#   deckhead above it        2.2 m of clear headroom the whole length
#   after bulkhead           drawn at x -6.265..-6.175, and OPEN between
#                            z = +0.16 and +1.08 from the sole to a header
#                            at 2.60 — a 0.92 m doorway with 1.98 m under it
#   capsule in `deeper_demo` 1.80 m tall, 0.70 m across (`height = 1.8`,
#                            `radius = 0.35`; 1.90 is the *default* shape and
#                            is not what that scene authors)
#
# So the door fits and the room fits, and the one solid box across the whole
# footprint was walling both of them up. `col_house` is now a shell — two
# sides, an after bulkhead with the door cut out of it, and a forward end.
#
# **`col_house_fwd` stays solid, and that is measured too.** The drawn console
# forward of x = 2.2 is 1.0 m proud of the sole and spans |z| <= 0.70, and the
# cabin side narrows to |z| = 0.86 there — so the passages either side of it
# are **0.14 m clear against a 0.70 m capsule**. The wheelhouse cannot be
# walked into however that block is authored. The salon can.
#
# `ponytail:` the settees, the tables and the console are **not** collided, so
# you walk through them. Colliding the settees costs two boxes and colliding
# the console costs the wheelhouse (see the 0.14 m above); this file's own rule
# is that boxes track *architecture*, and furniture is the half of the mesh
# still being detailed. Add them when somebody minds.
#
# `ponytail:` deck plates are rectangles and the bow is a wedge, so the two
# forward plates overhang the drawn hull by up to a metre of nothing. It is
# invisible and unreachable — the tapered bulwark boxes bound the walk and stop
# a capsule 0.1-0.2 m inboard of the drawn edge. Give the plates a yaw each if
# that margin ever has to be tighter.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "5f2c9a04-7b31-4e68-9d55-2a1e63b8c470"

# The instance node. It carries nothing itself: everything below hangs off it,
# and its transform is the identity so a collider's numbers in this file are
# the boat's own frame, exactly as they were measured.
[[node]]
name = "Decks"

[[node]]
name = "col_deck_aft"
parent = "Decks"
transform = { pos = [-7.8017, 0.49, 0.0], rot_euler = [0.0, 0.0, -0.655] }

  [node.components.BoxCollider]
  half_extents = [1.7501, 0.15, 2.62]

[[node]]
name = "col_deck_mid"
parent = "Decks"
transform = { pos = [-3.05, 0.465, 0.0] }

  [node.components.BoxCollider]
  half_extents = [3.10, 0.15, 2.82]

[[node]]
name = "col_deck_fwd"
parent = "Decks"
transform = { pos = [1.3025, 0.5125, 0.0], rot_euler = [0.0, 0.0, 0.955] }

  [node.components.BoxCollider]
  half_extents = [1.3502, 0.15, 2.82]

[[node]]
name = "col_deck_shoulder"
parent = "Decks"
transform = { pos = [3.506, 0.5751, 0.0], rot_euler = [0.0, 0.0, 2.291] }

  [node.components.BoxCollider]
  half_extents = [1.0008, 0.15, 2.55]

# **The foredeck is tilted slabs, not steps.** It climbs 0.765 -> 1.66 over
# 3.65 m, and cutting that into flat plates puts a 0.37 m riser at the forward
# end — over the step height, so the bow would be unreachable — and three seams
# for a camera to pop across.
#
# **Two slabs and not one, because the sheer is convex and a chord is not a
# curve.** A single 13.9 degree slab matched both ends to 5 mm and stood 0.29 m
# above the drawn deck in the middle, which is a person walking on air over the
# thing they appear to be standing on. Measured against the mesh, the drawn deck
# is 0.77 at x=4.5, 0.80 at 5.0, 0.84 at 5.5, 0.89 at 6.0, 0.98 at 6.5, 1.15 at
# 7.0, 1.37 at 7.5 and 1.65 at 8.0 — nearly flat aft and steep at the stem. Two
# chords hold it to under 5 cm everywhere and meet at the same height at x=6.4,
# so there is no step between them either.
[[node]]
name = "col_foredeck_aft"
parent = "Decks"
transform = { pos = [5.412, 0.6955, 0.0], rot_euler = [0.0, 0.0, 4.574] }

  [node.components.BoxCollider]
  half_extents = [1.0032, 0.15, 2.30]

[[node]]
name = "col_foredeck_fwd"
parent = "Decks"
transform = { pos = [7.286, 1.1555, 0.0], rot_euler = [0.0, 0.0, 24.019] }

  [node.components.BoxCollider]
  half_extents = [0.9032, 0.15, 1.30]

# --- bulwarks, following the taper -----------------------------------------
# Inner faces 0.1-0.2 m inboard of the drawn deck edge, so the capsule stops
# just short of the edge and never over it. The two forward pairs are yawed
# because the hull narrows from a 2.45 m half-beam to 0.50 m over six metres,
# and a straight box would either fence off the side deck or hang over the sea.
# Tops sit at the drawn cap height, not above it: a collider taller than the
# rail it represents is an invisible wall, which is the same lie as a missing
# one told the other way.

